      "The lesson is simple: a promise may become difficult to keep, but keeping it protects trust and brings lasting good. In an old market town, a merchant named Idris learned that a trustworthy word can become more valuable than the goods on his shelves.",
      "Before the winter rains, a teacher named Safiya visited Idris's store to order twenty wool blankets for the small school she managed. The school had saved carefully, so Idris promised a fair price and said, “They will be ready before the coldest week. You have my word.” Safiya paid a small deposit and returned to her village.",
      "A few days later, the road flooded and Idris's supplier could deliver only twenty blankets—the exact number he had promised. That afternoon, a travelling trader offered twice the price for all of them. Idris's assistant stared at the coins. “Sell them,” he urged. “When the road opens, we can buy more for the school.”",
      "Idris knew the extra money would repair his leaking roof and clear an old bill. For a long moment, the easy choice felt very close. Then he wrapped the twenty blankets for Safiya. “If my promise is worth nothing when it costs me,” he said, “then it was worth nothing when I made it.” He returned the trader's coins and sent the blankets to the school on his own cart.",
      "The cold week arrived early. Because Idris kept his word, every child had a warm blanket during lessons. Safiya told the children's families exactly what the merchant had done—not to advertise him, but to teach them what honesty looks like when it is difficult.",
      "In the months that followed, farmers, builders, and families began choosing Idris whenever they needed a reliable order. They knew his price would be fair and his promise would not change when a richer buyer entered the shop. The profit he had refused once returned slowly through years of honest dealing.",
