      "Weeks passed. The neighbours watched and shook their heads. Some pitied him; others called it stubbornness. Yusuf felt the same fear they did — he simply refused to let fear make his decisions for him. Each dawn he rose to work; each dusk he raised his hands: \"O Allah, I have done my small work. You are the Best of Providers.\"",
      "Then, in the seventh week, clouds gathered over the mountains like an answer. The rain fell for three nights — soft, steady, and sure. When the sun returned, Yusuf's field was the only one in the valley with green shoots standing ready to drink.",
      "But the story did not end there. Two of his neighbours' fields were still bare, and their families faced a lean season. Without being asked, Yusuf divided his early harvest among them. \"Patience,\" he told his son, \"was never only about my field. It was about keeping my heart generous even when my hands were empty.\"",
      "The neighbours came to thank him, and one asked, \"Yusuf, how did you keep working when you could not see the harvest?\" He answered, \"A seed trusts the soil it cannot see. Why should I trust Allah any less?\"",
